faqs:
  - question: "How do I deploy an Entire repository to Appwrite?"
    answer: "Go to **Project settings** > **Git configuration** in the Appwrite Console and click **Connect to Entire**. Approve the installation on entire.io and choose which repositories Appwrite can see. Then create a Site or Function, pick your Entire project and repository under **Import repository**, confirm the settings, and deploy. Every later push to that repository deploys automatically."
  - question: "Which Entire repositories can Appwrite access?"
    answer: "The ones you choose on the installation page on entire.io: **All repositories**, which includes repositories added to the project later, or **Only selected repositories**. Appwrite gets read access to repository contents and write access to checks. It cannot create or delete repositories on Entire. To change the selection later, use **Update Entire permissions** in the creation wizard or **Configure** on the installation in **Project settings** > **Git configuration**."
  - question: "Can one Appwrite project deploy from several Entire projects?"
    answer: "Yes. Each installation covers one Entire project. Click **Connect with Entire** in **Project settings** > **Git configuration** to add another, and each Entire project appears as its own owner in the repository picker."
  - question: "Do pushes to other branches deploy?"
    answer: "Yes, but they don't go live. A push to a branch other than the production branch creates a deployment that is built but not activated. For Sites, the deployment gets its own [preview URLs](/docs/products/sites/previews), which only members of your Appwrite organization can open."
  - question: "Why didn't my push to Entire create a deployment?"
    answer: "Check three things: the repository is connected to the Site or Function, the branch matches the branch filters under **Settings** > **Build** > **Triggers**, and at least one changed file matches the path filters. Path filters match from the repository root. The first push of a new branch has no previous commit to compare against, so it deploys regardless of path filters."
  - question: "Does Appwrite comment on Entire trails?"
    answer: "Not today. Appwrite reports each build to Entire as a check run on the commit instead, and the deployment itself, with its logs and preview, lives in the Appwrite Console."
---

Entire keeps your code and the agent sessions behind it in one repository. Appwrite runs that code. With Entire now an Appwrite Git provider, you can connect the two once and let every push deploy.

This guide deploys a React web app with [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites) and a Node.js API with [Appwrite Functions](/docs/products/functions) from a single Entire repository. By the end, pushes to `main` update production, pushes to other branches produce private previews, and path filters keep the site and the function from rebuilding each other.

# How deploying from Entire to Appwrite works

To deploy from Entire, connect your Entire project to Appwrite from **Project settings** > **Git configuration**, approve the installation on entire.io, then create a Site or Function from one of the project's repositories. From then on, every push to that repository triggers a build:

- **Production branch:** the deployment builds and activates.
- **Any other branch:** the deployment builds but stays inactive. Sites get preview URLs for the branch and the commit.
- **Every build:** unless Silent mode is on, Appwrite reports the result back to Entire as a check run on the commit.

Under the hood, Appwrite installs as a plugin on your Entire project. Entire notifies Appwrite of each push, Appwrite fetches that commit through Entire's own Git tooling, builds it, and reports the outcome back.

# Prerequisites

- An [Appwrite Cloud](/sign-in) account with a project.
- An Entire account and an organization you can create projects in.
- The [Entire CLI](https://docs.entire.com/installation), installed and signed in with `entire login`. Its installer also adds `git-remote-entire`, the helper Git calls for `entire://` remotes, so plain `git push` works.
- Node.js, if you want to build the example site locally.

# Step 1: Create an Entire project and repository

Appwrite can't create repositories on Entire, so the repository has to exist before you connect it.

Entire repositories live inside projects. A project has an owner, usually your organization, and a primary region where new repositories are created by default. Each Appwrite installation connects to one Entire project, so keep the repositories you plan to deploy in the same project when you can.

Create both from the CLI:

```bash
entire login
entire project create acme --owner acme-org
entire repo create storefront --project acme
entire repo view /et/acme/storefront
```

Project names are 3 to 32 characters of lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, and they must be unique across Entire. `entire repo view` prints the repository's region and its clone URL, which uses Entire's `entire://` scheme:

```txt
CLUSTER                     ROLE     CLONE URL                                                STATUS
aws-eu-central-1.entire.io  primary  entire://aws-eu-central-1.entire.io/et/acme/storefront  ready
```

You can do the same on entire.io: create a project from your organization, then a repository inside it. The repository's **Clone** menu shows the command for a fresh clone and the one for connecting a project you already have. Entire's [repository docs](https://docs.entire.com/guides/repositories/entire-native-repositories) cover regions and regional mirrors in more detail.

![The Clone menu of an Entire repository with commands to clone it or connect an existing project](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/entire-clone.avif)

## Push your code

This guide uses one repository for both the site and the function. The React site sits at the root, which lets Appwrite detect the framework on its own, and the function lives in its own folder:

```txt
storefront/
├── index.html
├── package.json        # React and Vite site
├── vite.config.js
├── src/
│   ├── App.jsx
│   └── main.jsx
└── functions/
    └── api/            # Node.js function
        ├── package.json
        └── src/
            └── main.js
```

The function can start small. Appwrite calls the default export with a context object that carries the request, the response, and logging helpers:

```js
// functions/api/src/main.js
export default async ({ req, res, log }) => {
  log(`Handling ${req.method} ${req.path}`);
  return res.json({ ok: true, path: req.path });
};
```

Mark the function's package as an ES module so the `export default` syntax works:

```json
{
  "name": "api",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "src/main.js"
}
```

From your local project, point `origin` at the Entire repository and push:

```bash
entire repo remote add origin --override /et/acme/storefront
git push -u origin main
```

A plain `git remote add origin <clone URL>` works too, since `git-remote-entire` handles the `entire://` address.

# Step 2: Connect Entire to your Appwrite project

1. In the Appwrite Console, open your project and go to **Project settings** > **Git configuration**.
2. Click **Connect to Entire**. If the project already has a Git installation, the button reads **Connect with Entire**.

![The Git configuration card of an Appwrite project with a Connect to Entire button](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/git-configuration.avif)

3. entire.io opens Appwrite's installation page. Choose your Entire project, then pick the access Appwrite gets: **All repositories**, which also covers repositories you add to the project later, or **Only selected repositories**, here `storefront`.
4. Click **Install**. Entire sends you back to the Console.

![Entire's Install Appwrite page with the acme project selected and the storefront repository checked](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/entire-install.avif)

The installation page states exactly what Appwrite can do: read repository contents and write checks on commits. It cannot create or delete repositories. Back in the Console, your Entire project appears in the **Owner** column of the installation list.

![The Git configuration card listing the acme Entire project as an installation](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/git-installation.avif)

Approval is per Entire project, so to deploy from a second project, click **Connect with Entire** again. You can also start from the Site or Function creation wizard: when the project has no Git installation yet, **Import repository** shows a **Connect Entire** button that runs the same flow.

# Step 3: Deploy a site from your Entire repository

1. Go to **Sites** and click **Create site**.
2. Under **Import repository**, open the organization menu and choose your Entire project. Entire projects are marked with the Entire icon.
3. Find `storefront` and click **Connect**.

![The Import repository list of the Create site wizard showing the storefront repository from the acme Entire project](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/create-site-import.avif)

4. Enter a **Site name**, such as `storefront-web`, and pick a **Domain** on `appwrite.network`.
5. Confirm the **Framework**. Appwrite reads the `package.json` in the root directory, detects React, and fills in the install command, build command, and output directory. The **Branch** is `main` and the **Root directory** is `./`.
6. Add any environment variables your app needs under **Environment variables**.
7. Click **Deploy**.

![The Create site form with React detected from the acme/storefront Entire repository](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/site-configure.avif)

Appwrite fetches the commit from Entire, runs the install and build commands, and serves the output on the site's domain. Because `main` is the production branch, this first deployment activates as soon as the build succeeds. In our test, the build took 15 seconds.

![The deployed storefront site with its source shown as acme/storefront on the main branch](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/site-deployed.avif)

# Step 4: Push to deploy

Make a change to the site, commit it, and push to `main`:

```bash
git commit -am "Update the landing page copy"
git push
```

Entire notifies Appwrite of the push. Appwrite creates a deployment, builds it, and activates it, replacing the previous production deployment. The site's **Deployments** tab shows the new active deployment, with the Entire repository as its source.

![The active deployment of the storefront-web site with the updated copy and acme/storefront as the source](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/active-deployment.avif)

Each deployment links back to Entire: clicking its commit message opens that commit on entire.io. Appwrite also reports the build to Entire as a check run on the commit, named after your site and your Appwrite project, with the result and a link to the deployment.

# Step 5: Preview a branch before it ships

Push a branch other than `main`:

```bash
git switch -c redesign-hero
# edit src/App.jsx
git commit -am "Try a new hero section"
git push -u origin redesign-hero
```

Appwrite builds this deployment but does not activate it, so production keeps serving the previous version. In the **Deployments** list, the branch deployment shows as **Ready** while the `main` deployment stays **Active**.

![The deployments list with the redesign-hero deployment ready and the main deployment active](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/deployments-list.avif)

Click the deployment to open it. Its panel shows a preview of the branch, plus URLs for the branch and the commit. Live traffic keeps using the active deployment until you click **Activate**.

![A branch deployment's preview and its branch and commit URLs](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/preview-deployment.avif)

[Preview deployments](/docs/products/sites/previews) are private. Opening one requires an Appwrite account in the organization that owns the site, and the first time you open one, Appwrite asks you to authorize **Appwrite Preview** so it can confirm your membership. When the branch is ready, merge it into `main` and push to deploy it to production.

# Step 6: Deploy a function from the same repository

1. Go to **Functions** and click **Create function**.
2. Under **Connect Git repository**, choose your Entire project, find `storefront`, and click **Connect**.
3. Enter a **Function name**, such as `api`. Appwrite detects the Node.js **Runtime** from the repository.
4. Check **Public**. It's on by default and lets anyone execute the function, so turn it off if the function should only run for signed-in users or server calls.
5. Under **Production branch**, keep `main` and set the **Root directory** to `functions/api`.
6. Under **Build**, set the **Entrypoint** to `src/main.js` and keep `npm install` as the **Build command**.
7. Click **Create and deploy**.

![The Create function form with functions/api as the root directory and src/main.js as the entrypoint](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/function-configure.avif)

Once the build finishes, call the function's domain to check it:

```bash
curl https://<your-function-domain>/hello
# {"ok":true,"path":"/hello"}
```

From now on, pushes to `main` create a function deployment that activates once it builds. Pushes to other branches create deployments that stay inactive until you activate them. For more on entrypoints, runtimes, and the context object, see [Develop Appwrite Functions](/docs/products/functions/develop).

# Step 7: Scope builds with path filters

With a site and a function in one repository, every push now builds both. A copy change to the site shouldn't rebuild your API, and a fix in `functions/api` shouldn't redeploy your site. [Build triggers](/blog/post/announcing-git-deployment-triggers) fix that.

For the site, go to **Sites** > your site > **Settings** > **Build**. Under **Triggers**, add a path filter that excludes the function's folder, then click **Update**:

```txt
!functions/**
```

![The Triggers card of a site with the !functions/** path filter](/images/blog/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite/site-path-filter.avif)

For the function, go to **Functions** > your function > **Settings** > **Build** and add a path filter for its own folder:

```txt
functions/api/**
```

Path filters match from the repository root, not from the root directory you set. Branch filters work the same way: add patterns such as `main` and `preview/**` to limit which branches create deployments at all.

Appwrite works out which files a push changed by comparing the push's previous and new commits on Entire. In our test, a push that only touched `functions/api` deployed and activated the function and left the site alone. The first push of a new branch has nothing to compare against, so it deploys regardless of path filters. After that, the filters apply to every push on the branch.

# Manage the Entire connection over time

- **Add repositories.** If a repository is missing from the picker, it isn't covered by the installation. Click **Update Entire permissions** under **Can't find a repository?** in the creation wizard, or open the installation's menu in **Project settings** > **Git configuration** and click **Configure**. Both reopen Appwrite's installation page on entire.io. Choosing **All repositories** there covers new repositories automatically.
- **Disconnect from Appwrite.** Open the installation's menu in **Project settings** > **Git configuration** and click **Disconnect**. The dialog lists the Sites and Functions that use the installation before you confirm.
- **Uninstall from Entire.** If you remove Appwrite from your Entire project, Appwrite disconnects every Site and Function that deployed from it and removes the installation. Their current deployments keep serving, but new pushes stop deploying.

# Troubleshooting Entire deployments

- **The repository isn't listed.** Add it to the installation as described above, then click **Refresh repositories** in the wizard.
- **There is no option to create a repository.** The wizard only offers **Create new repository** for providers that support it. For Entire, create the repository on Entire first, push your code, and connect it.
- **The framework shows as Other.** Appwrite detects the framework from the `package.json` in the root directory. If your app lives in a subfolder, set the **Root directory** to that folder, and if Appwrite still doesn't recognize the framework, select it yourself.
- **No check runs appear on Entire.** Silent mode stops Appwrite from reporting builds back to Entire. Turn it off in the site or function's Git settings.
- **The build fails.** Open the deployment in the Console and read the build logs. Most failures come from a root directory that doesn't contain the app, or a build command that doesn't match the package manager.

# Getting started with Entire on Appwrite Cloud

Once the installation is in place, deploying from Entire looks like any other Git workflow on Appwrite: push to `main` to ship, push a branch to preview, and use path filters to keep a monorepo's builds independent. The difference is that the repository also keeps the agent sessions that produced each change, so the commit, its history, and its running deployment are all one click apart.

- [Sign up for Appwrite Cloud](/sign-in) to connect your first Entire project.
- [Deploy Sites from Git](/docs/products/sites/deploy-from-git) covers build triggers, install and build commands, and output directories.
- [Deploy Functions from Git](/docs/products/functions/deploy-from-git) covers the same ground for serverless functions.
- [Preview deployments](/docs/products/sites/previews) explains how branch previews are created and who can open them.
